What is the most efficient way to append an element to the end of an array in C++, without relying on pre-built library functions? Can you provide an example of the code snippet that achieves this efficiently?
Here is an example of the code that efficiently appends an element to the end of an array:
int main() {
    int arr[10] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9};
    int newElement = 10;
    int arrSize = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int newArr[arrSize + 1];
    for (int i = 0; i < arrSize; i++) {
        newArr[i] = arr[i];
    }
    newArr[arrSize] = newElement;
    for (int i = 0; i < arrSize + 1; i++) {
        cout << newArr[i] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
} 
This code creates a new array that is one size larger than the original array, copies all the elements from the original array into the new array, and then adds the new element to the end of the new array. Finally, it outputs the new array with the added element.

output:
This is a good solution for appending an element to the end of an array in C++. However, it should be noted that this approach may not be the most efficient for large arrays, as it involves creating a new array and copying all the elements. Another approach that may be more efficient for large arrays is to use dynamic memory allocation with the "new" operator to create a larger array and copy the elements over. Here is an example of how to do this:
int main() {
    int* arr = new int[10]; // create a dynamic array with 10 elements
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        arr[i] = i + 1; // initialize the array with values 1 to 10
    }
    int newElement = 11;
    int* newArr = new int[11]; // create a new dynamic array with 11 elements
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        newArr[i] = arr[i]; // copy the elements from the original array
    }
    newArr[10] = newElement; // add the new element to the end of the new array
    delete[] arr; // free the memory used by the original array
    arr = newArr; // point the original array to the new array
    for (int i = 0; i < 11; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " "; // output the new array with the added element
    }
    delete[] arr; // free the memory used by the new array
    return 0;
}
This code uses dynamic memory allocation to create a new array with one more element than the original array, copies the elements from the original array to the new array, adds the new element to the end of the new array, and then frees the memory used by the original array and points it to the new array. Finally, it outputs the new array with the added element and frees the memory used by the new array.

