        {
            thesisStatement = "The author uses George's characterization and imagery to convey the theme of unnoticed selflessness in the face of adversity.";
        }
        else
        {
            thesisStatement = "Unable to generate a thesis statement based on the given passage.";
        }
        Console.WriteLine(thesisStatement);
    }
}
```
In this example, we first define the passage as a string variable. We then use regular expressions to extract information about George's characterization, specifically the number of times the words "selfless," "hardworking," "unnoticed," and "invisible" appear in the passage.
Based on this information, we generate a thesis statement that reflects the theme of the passage. In this case, the thesis statement is "The author uses George's characterization and imagery to convey the theme of unnoticed selflessness in the face of adversity."
While this example is rudimentary and doesn't perform a full analysis of the passage, it demonstrates how C# can be used to extract specific information and generate a thesis statement based on that information.

