Here is an example function in C++ to find the maximum element in an array:

```
#include <iostream>

int findMax(int arr[], int n) {
    int max = arr[0];
    for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if(arr[i] > max) {
            max = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return max;
}

int main() {
    int array[] = {8, 3, 5, 9, 3, 1, 5};
    int n = sizeof(array)/sizeof(array[0]);
    int max = findMax(array, n);
    std::cout << "Max element is: " << max << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

The `findMax` function takes an integer array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs. It initializes the `max` variable to the first element of the array. Then it iterates through the array and compares each element with `max`. If an element is greater than `max`, it replaces `max` with that element. Finally, the function returns `max`.

In the `main` function, we declare the `array` and get its size `n`. We then call `findMax` with `array` and `n` as inputs and store the result in `max`. Finally, we print the result to the console.

