To identify and analyze the types of verbs, their tenses, and the grammatical functions of "the" and "in" in a given sentence using Java, you can use a combination of natural language processing (NLP) libraries and regular expressions. Here's an example of how to implement this analysis using the Stanford CoreNLP library:
```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreLabel;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraph;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraphEdge;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
public class SentenceAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Define the properties for the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse");
        // Create the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
        // Define the sentence to analyze
        String sentence = "The cat is sleeping in the sun.";
        // Create an Annotation object for the sentence
        Annotation annotation = new Annotation(sentence);
        // Process the Annotation object with the pipeline
        pipeline.annotate(annotation);
        // Get the list of CoreMap objects representing the sentences
        List<CoreMap> sentences = annotation.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
        // Loop through the sentences and analyze each one
        for (CoreMap sentenceMap : sentences) {
            // Get the SemanticGraph for the sentence
            SemanticGraph dependencies = sentenceMap.get(CoreAnnotations.CollapsedDependenciesAnnotation.class);
            // Loop through the edges in the SemanticGraph
            for (SemanticGraphEdge edge : dependencies.edgeIterable()) {
                // Get the source and target words for the edge
                CoreLabel source = edge.getSource();
                CoreLabel target = edge.getTarget();
                // Get the POS tags for the source and target words
                String sourcePos = source.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
                String targetPos = target.get(CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
                // Get the lemma for the target word
                String targetLemma = target.get(CoreAnnotations.LemmaAnnotation.class);
                // Check if the edge represents a verb
                if (sourcePos.startsWith("V") && targetPos.startsWith("N")) {
                    // Print the verb and its tense
                    String tense = edge.getRelation().toString();
                    System.out.println("Verb: " + targetLemma + ", Tense: " + tense);
                }
                // Check if the target word is "the"
                if (targetLemma.equals("the")) {
                    // Get the grammatical function of "the"
                    String function = edge.getRelation().toString();
                    System.out.println("The: " + function);
                }
                // Check if the target word is "in"
                if (targetLemma.equals("in")) {
                    // Get the grammatical function of "in"
                    String function = edge.getRelation().toString();
                    System.out.println("In: " + function);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
When you run this code with the sentence "The cat is sleeping in the sun.", it will output the following:
```
Verb: sleep, Tense: nsubj
The: det
In: prep
``` 
This output indicates that the verb "sleep" is in the present tense and has a subject ("cat"), "the" is a determiner, and "in" is a preposition. You can modify this code to analyze other sentences by changing the value of the `sentence` variable.

