To analyze the distribution of words in a given HTML page using Go, you can utilize the net/http and ioutil packages to perform a GET request and extract the text from the response body. Once you have the text, you can use regular expressions to split it into individual words and calculate the frequency of each word.
Here is a sample Go code that demonstrates this process:
```
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"net/http"
	"regexp"
	"strings"
)
func main() {
	// Make a GET request to the URL of the HTML page
	resp, err := http.Get("https://example.com")
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()
	// Read the response body
	body, err := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	// Convert the body to a string
	bodyString := string(body)
	// Use regular expressions to extract text from the body
	re := regexp.MustCompile(`(?s)<.*?>`)
	bodyString = re.ReplaceAllString(bodyString, " ")
	// Split the text into words
	words := strings.Fields(bodyString)
	// Calculate the frequency of each word
	frequency := make(map[string]int)
	for _, word := range words {
		frequency[word]++
	}
	// Print the frequency of each word
	for word, count := range frequency {
		fmt.Printf("%s: %d\n", word, count)
	}
}
```
This code makes a GET request to the URL of the HTML page, reads the response body, and extracts the text using regular expressions. It then splits the text into individual words and calculates the frequency of each word using a map. Finally, it prints out the frequency of each word.
The output of this code will be a map containing the frequency of each word as keys and values respectively.

