To add a new row to the table with atomicity, you can use a transaction. A transaction is a sequence of SQL statements that are executed as a single unit of work. If any of the statements in the transaction fail, the entire transaction is rolled back, ensuring that the database is left in a consistent state.
Here is an example SQL command that adds a new row to the table with atomicity:
```
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
DECLARE @type int;
SELECT @type = MAX(type) + 1 FROM table;
INSERT INTO table (id, type, information) VALUES (NULL, @type, 'W');
COMMIT TRANSACTION;
```
This command starts a transaction using `BEGIN TRANSACTION`, then selects the highest type value from the table and adds 1 to it to get the new type value. Finally, it inserts a new row into the table with the new type value and "W" as the information value. The transaction is then committed using `COMMIT TRANSACTION`.
To handle concurrency in database operations, there are several strategies you can use. One is to use locking to prevent multiple transactions from modifying the same data at the same time. Another is to use optimistic concurrency control, where each transaction checks for conflicts before committing changes. Additionally, you can use isolation levels to control the degree to which transactions are isolated from each other. The higher the isolation level, the less likely it is that two transactions will conflict with each other.

To handle arrays with outliers and partial sorting, we can modify the QuickSort algorithm by using a variation of the median-of-three partitioning scheme. This scheme chooses the median of the first, middle, and last elements of the array as the pivot. This ensures that the pivot is closer to the true median of the array and reduces the likelihood of picking an outlier as the pivot.
To further improve performance for larger datasets, we can implement a hybrid sorting algorithm that switches to a different sorting algorithm, such as MergeSort, when the subarrays become smaller than a certain threshold. This can help reduce the number of recursive calls and improve overall performance.
In terms of data structures, we can use a stack to simulate the recursive calls of the QuickSort algorithm. This can help reduce the overhead of function calls and improve performance.
Here is sample code in Python that implements the modified QuickSort algorithm with hybrid sorting:
```
import random
def quicksort(arr, start, end):
    if start >= end:
        return
    if end - start < 10:
        # Switch to insertion sort for small subarrays
        insertionsort(arr, start, end)
        return
    pivot = median_of_three(arr, start, end)
    i = start
    j = end - 1
    while i <= j:
        while arr[i] < pivot:
            i += 1
        while arr[j] > pivot:
            j -= 1
        if i <= j:
            arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
            i += 1
            j -= 1
    quicksort(arr, start, j + 1)
    quicksort(arr, i, end)
def median_of_three(arr, start, end):
    mid = (start + end) // 2
    if arr[start] > arr[mid]:
        arr[start], arr[mid] = arr[mid], arr[start]
    if arr[start] > arr[end]:
        arr[start], arr[end] = arr[end], arr[start]
    if arr[mid] > arr[end]:
        arr[mid], arr[end] = arr[end], arr[mid]
    arr[mid], arr[end - 1] = arr[end - 1], arr[mid]
    return arr[end - 1]
def insertionsort(arr, start, end):
    for i in range(start + 1, end):
        j = i
        while j > start and arr[j] < arr[j - 1]:
            arr[j], arr[j - 1] = arr[j - 1], arr[j]
            j -= 1
